     - Cause (`Region::carve`, bank branch): a river only shaped the ground up to `max(5, 2 * width)` blocks from its channel, and the bank was cut only where the terrain stood at most 10 blocks over its slope (`s - target <= 10`). Beside a hill the slope stopped 5 blocks out at `ws + 3`, so the terrain there was either left whole (more than 10 over: a sheer wall from the channel's edge) or cut and left standing as a wall of up to 10 at the end of the band. The water level (grade of the 16-block grid) is often far under the real terrain of a column, so this showed everywhere there was a hill.
     - Fix (`hydro.rs`, one function and two consts): `reach(w)` = `max(VALLEY 48, 2 w)` is how far a river still shapes the ground (`Network::plan` and `index_of` use it; `Plan::blend` and the flowers keep the old band). The bank rises `BANK_SLOPE` 0.5 a block from the waterline (was 0.75, only to the band) and over the outer half of the reach eases into the terrain's own height (smoothstep), so the cut ends flush with the land: a hill beside a river is a valley side. The 10 block limit is gone. Low ground (the levee) is unchanged.
